{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 10, "high": 14, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 14, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.6,
      "why": "iterative is the only family that can plausibly meet throughput_msps >= 1 while minimising area. With 1 result per (N+3) cycles, throughput = Fmax/(N+3). To hit >= 1 MSPS we need Fmax >= N+3 MHz. For N=10..14, Fmax must be >= 13..17 MHz, which is very relaxed for sky130 standard cells. Area scales with data_width and n_iter, so we focus on the minimum widths that satisfy max_abs_err <= 2^-10. Accuracy depends on n_iter and guards; N=10..14 with appropriate guards should give >= 10 bits accuracy. data_width 10..14 covers the minimum needed for 10-bit accuracy (output LSB is 2^-(W-2), so W=12 gives LSB=2^-10, but we need max_abs_err <= 2^-10, so W=12 with N>=10 and rounding should work; W=10..14 explores the trade-off). angle_guard 0..2 and frac_guard 0..2 keep the datapath narrow while allowing enough precision for the CORDIC iterations to converge to 10-bit accuracy. rounding=round is likely better for accuracy at the same width, but trunc is cheaper; include both."
    },
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 10, "high": 14, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 14, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 4, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.4,
      "why": "unrolled_k with k=2..4 reduces latency to ceil(N/k)+3 cycles, improving throughput to Fmax/(ceil(N/k)+3). For N=10..14 and k=2..4, latency is 5..10 cycles, so throughput = Fmax/5..Fmax/10. This gives more Fmax headroom than iterative, but the unrolled datapath has k parallel adders/shifters, increasing area. The trade-off is: higher k -> better throughput but more area. Since area is the primary objective and the throughput constraint is only >= 1 MSPS (very relaxed), unrolled_k may not beat iterative on area unless the Fmax constraint forces iterative to use wider datapaths or more iterations. Include k=2..4 to explore whether modest unrolling can reduce n_iter or data_width while still meeting throughput, potentially lowering area. Same data_width, n_iter, guard ranges as iterative since accuracy requirements are identical."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec requires >= 1 MSPS throughput and max_abs_err <= 2^-10, with area as the primary objective. Both iterative and unrolled_k can meet the throughput constraint easily since the required Fmax is low (13-17 MHz for iterative, 5-10 MHz for unrolled_k with k=2-4). The key trade-off is between area (driven by data_width, n_iter, and datapath width) and accuracy (driven by n_iter, guards, and rounding). I focus data_width on 10-14 because the output LSB is 2^-(W-2), so W=12 gives LSB=2^-10, and with N>=10 iterations and rounding, max_abs_err should be <= 2^-10. n_iter 10-14 is the minimum range that should achieve 10-bit accuracy with modest guards. angle_guard and frac_guard are kept small (0-2) to minimise datapath width while allowing the CORDIC iterations to converge. I allocate 60% of the budget to iterative since it has the simplest datapath (single adder/shift chain) and is likely to have the lowest area for a given accuracy, and 40% to unrolled_k to explore whether modest unrolling (k=2-4) can reduce the required n_iter or data_width while still meeting throughput, potentially finding a lower-area point on the Pareto front. The hypervolume reference point (area=40000 um^2, accuracy_bits=10) suggests that designs with area well below 40000 and accuracy >= 10 bits will contribute most to the hypervolume, so the search should focus on the low-area, just-meets-accuracy region."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "The feasible Pareto front is established with 3 designs, all meeting the strict constraints: throughput >= 1 MSPS (best 8.32-8.68 MSPS) and max_abs_err <= 2^-10 (best 10.05-10.45 bits). The minimum area design is iterative [data_width=14, n_iter=14, angle_guard=1, frac_guard=2, rounding=trunc] at 7014 um^2. Since this is the final round (budget/cap reached), and the front covers the relevant trade-off space for the 'min area' selection rule, we stop. The selection rule 'min area_um2' will pick the 7014 um^2 design."
}
